 ],
 "text": "Your touchpad can't see your finger. It feels it stealing electricity. So how does that work? Under the surface sits a grid of copper wires. Rows one way, columns the other. Every crossing is a tiny capacitor, a spot that holds charge. Now Sofia rests one finger on the pad. Her skin conducts, so it pulls a little charge away from the crossings underneath. Then the chip checks every crossing, up to two hundred times a second, and hunts for the dip. But a typical pad has just sixteen wires across. So it compares how much each wire lost, and pins her finger to one nine-hundred-sixtieth of an inch. Now she clicks. On a MacBook, that click is fake. The pad never moves. Four force sensors feel the press, and a magnet taps back sideways. Her brain fills in the click. It all started in nineteen ninety-four, with Cirque's GlidePoint in the PowerBook five hundred. So your touchpad is a grid that feels charge vanish, and a fake click that fools your brain. Follow can we dot A I for more breakdowns like this."
